1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:03,221
There's a famous quote about bees.

2
00:00:03,551 --> 00:00:05,851
If the bee disappeared from the face of the Earth,

3
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:08,784
humans would have only four years left to live.

4
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:11,659
It's usually signed: Albert Einstein.

5
00:00:12,009 --> 00:00:13,389
There's just one problem.

6
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:15,111
Einstein never said it.

7
00:00:15,851 --> 00:00:19,028
The earliest version anyone has found appeared

8
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:21,371
in a French magazine in 1965.

9
00:00:21,651 --> 00:00:23,357
Ten years after he died.

10
00:00:23,737 --> 00:00:25,386
But the question behind the fake

11
00:00:25,406 --> 00:00:26,727
quote is a real one.

12
00:00:26,977 --> 00:00:29,752
What would actually happen if every bee disappeared?

13
00:00:30,372 --> 00:00:31,752
The true answer is less like

14
00:00:31,772 --> 00:00:33,062
the end of the world,

15
00:00:33,242 --> 00:00:36,085
and more like a very strange trip to the supermarket.

16
00:00:41,325 --> 00:00:42,225
Chapter one.

17
00:00:42,245 --> 00:00:43,753
What a bee actually does.

18
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:46,643
A bee doesn't visit flowers to help us.

19
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:47,733
She's shopping.

20
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:49,073
Nectar for energy,

21
00:00:49,093 --> 00:00:50,712
and pollen to feed her young.

22
00:00:51,042 --> 00:00:52,472
But pollen is sticky,

23
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:53,772
and bees are fuzzy.

24
00:00:54,042 --> 00:00:55,162
So on every visit,

25
00:00:55,182 --> 00:00:56,775
a little golden dust from one

26
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:58,771
flower rides along to the next.

27
00:00:59,101 --> 00:01:00,381
That's pollination.

28
00:01:00,671 --> 00:01:03,291
When pollen reaches a flower of the same kind,

29
00:01:03,361 --> 00:01:05,331
the flower can swell into a fruit,

30
00:01:05,351 --> 00:01:06,767
with seeds inside.

31
00:01:07,107 --> 00:01:11,117
No delivery, and many plants give smaller fruit, misshapen fruit,

32
00:01:11,147 --> 00:01:12,479
or no fruit at all.

33
00:01:12,809 --> 00:01:14,729
And bees are not one animal.

34
00:01:15,089 --> 00:01:18,149
There are about twenty thousand species of wild bees,

35
00:01:18,249 --> 00:01:20,133
from big fuzzy bumblebees to bees

36
00:01:20,153 --> 00:01:22,139
smaller than a grain of rice.

37
00:01:22,519 --> 00:01:23,941
The honeybee on your jar of

38
00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:25,675
honey is just one of them.

39
00:01:26,845 --> 00:01:27,705
Chapter two.

40
00:01:27,725 --> 00:01:29,251
The supermarket test.

41
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:32,200
Imagine walking into a supermarket on the

42
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:34,525
morning after the last bee is gone.

43
00:01:34,875 --> 00:01:36,319
Scientists who studied the world's leading food crops found

44
00:01:36,339 --> 00:01:37,925
that eighty-seven of them depend on animal pollination,

45
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:42,575
at least partly.

46
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:44,547
Only twenty-eight don't.

47
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:46,077
Counted by type,

48
00:01:46,197 --> 00:01:47,836
more than three quarters of the

49
00:01:47,856 --> 00:01:50,027
world's food crops rely on pollinators,

50
00:01:50,097 --> 00:01:51,207
at least partly.

51
00:01:51,567 --> 00:01:52,317
Together,

52
00:01:52,417 --> 00:01:54,030
they're worth up to five hundred

53
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:56,382
and seventy-seven billion dollars a year.

54
00:01:56,732 --> 00:01:58,581
So start in the fruit aisle.

55
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:01,109
Apples, pears, cherries, melons, blueberries.

56
00:02:01,129 --> 00:02:02,043
Fewer of them,

57
00:02:02,063 --> 00:02:03,972
and much more expensive.

58
00:02:04,322 --> 00:02:05,502
Then vegetables.

59
00:02:05,712 --> 00:02:07,402
Pumpkins, cucumbers,

60
00:02:07,422 --> 00:02:09,052
and even the seeds farmers need

61
00:02:09,072 --> 00:02:10,794
to grow carrots and onions.

62
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:12,554
Nuts take a heavy hit,

63
00:02:12,574 --> 00:02:13,874
almonds most of all.

64
00:02:14,114 --> 00:02:16,357
And your coffee gets a little harder to grow.

65
00:02:16,707 --> 00:02:19,233
But now, look at the other side of the store.

66
00:02:19,963 --> 00:02:21,769
Bread, rice, pasta, cornflakes.

67
00:02:21,789 --> 00:02:25,022
Wheat, rice and maize are pollinated by the wind.

68
00:02:25,042 --> 00:02:27,329
They don't need a single bee.

69
00:02:27,679 --> 00:02:32,589
That's why, by sheer weight,

70
00:02:32,609 --> 00:02:33,312
about sixty percent of the world's food comes

71
00:02:33,332 --> 00:02:34,249
from crops that don't need animals at all.

72
00:02:34,679 --> 00:02:37,351
Thirty-five percent comes from crops that do.

73
00:02:37,701 --> 00:02:39,531
So we wouldn't run out of calories.

74
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:40,951
We'd run out of colour.

75
00:02:41,211 --> 00:02:42,706
The foods that fade away are

76
00:02:42,726 --> 00:02:44,557
the ones packed with vitamins.

77
00:02:45,737 --> 00:02:46,567
Chapter three.

78
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:47,792
The human bees.

79
00:02:48,462 --> 00:02:49,872
To see what that looks like,

80
00:02:49,982 --> 00:02:52,745
travel to the mountains of Sichuan, in China.

81
00:02:53,075 --> 00:02:54,385
In Maoxian County,

82
00:02:54,435 --> 00:02:56,795
apple and pear trees bloom every spring.

83
00:02:57,085 --> 00:02:57,905
But for years,

84
00:02:57,925 --> 00:02:59,482
not enough insects came.

85
00:02:59,892 --> 00:03:03,602
Farmers had sprayed pesticides eight to ten times a season,

86
00:03:03,722 --> 00:03:05,212
for about four decades.

87
00:03:05,552 --> 00:03:07,292
And the forests and grasslands around

88
00:03:07,312 --> 00:03:09,419
the valley were turning into farmland.

89
00:03:09,749 --> 00:03:11,572
So, from the late nineteen-eighties,

90
00:03:11,592 --> 00:03:14,363
people started doing the bees' job themselves.

91
00:03:14,703 --> 00:03:16,173
They collected flowers,

92
00:03:16,193 --> 00:03:17,783
built up a stock of pollen,

93
00:03:17,943 --> 00:03:19,462
and climbed into the trees to

94
00:03:19,482 --> 00:03:21,013
dab it onto the blossoms,

95
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:22,124
one by one.

96
00:03:22,454 --> 00:03:25,308
A survey in 2001 found that every single

97
00:03:25,328 --> 00:03:28,434
apple in Maoxian was pollinated by hand.

98
00:03:28,774 --> 00:03:30,234
And the clock is brutal.

99
00:03:30,484 --> 00:03:32,279
The flowers have to be pollinated

100
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:34,467
within about five days of opening.

101
00:03:34,807 --> 00:03:37,641
One person can do five to ten trees a day.

102
00:03:37,991 --> 00:03:40,171
As young workers left for the cities,

103
00:03:40,331 --> 00:03:42,981
the cost of that labour rose about tenfold,

104
00:03:43,091 --> 00:03:45,977
to twelve to nineteen dollars per person, per day.

105
00:03:46,317 --> 00:03:48,087
Many farmers gave up on apples,

106
00:03:48,127 --> 00:03:49,337
and switched to plums,

107
00:03:49,357 --> 00:03:51,007
loquats and walnuts.

108
00:03:51,337 --> 00:03:52,387
It made sense.

109
00:03:52,547 --> 00:03:55,287
Apples were selling for less than twenty cents a kilo.

110
00:03:55,487 --> 00:03:57,531
Plums fetched around two dollars.

111
00:03:57,881 --> 00:03:59,572
Humans can do a bee's job.

112
00:03:59,592 --> 00:04:01,062
Just slowly, expensively,

113
00:04:01,082 --> 00:04:02,603
and not for long.

114
00:04:04,383 --> 00:04:05,214
Chapter four.

115
00:04:05,234 --> 00:04:07,505
One valley, almost every hive.

116
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:10,055
Now look at the opposite approach.

117
00:04:10,095 --> 00:04:11,199
California.

118
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:13,179
Most of the world's almonds grow

119
00:04:13,199 --> 00:04:14,759
in one long valley there.

120
00:04:14,949 --> 00:04:16,999
And almond orchards need bees.

121
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:19,301
About two hives for every acre.

122
00:04:19,651 --> 00:04:20,791
So every winter,

123
00:04:20,921 --> 00:04:23,201
beekeepers load their hives onto trucks,

124
00:04:23,291 --> 00:04:26,038
and drive them in from all over the United States.

125
00:04:26,408 --> 00:04:28,348
For the 2024 bloom,

126
00:04:28,478 --> 00:04:32,048
growers needed around two point seven million colonies.

127
00:04:32,408 --> 00:04:34,390
That's roughly as many as the entire

128
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:36,956
country counted on the first of January.

129
00:04:37,296 --> 00:04:38,740
The average rent was about a

130
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:40,766
hundred and eighty dollars per hive,

131
00:04:40,866 --> 00:04:42,199
for a few weeks of work.

132
00:04:42,539 --> 00:04:46,648
Almond pollination alone earned beekeepers more than three

133
00:04:46,668 --> 00:04:49,999
hundred and twenty million dollars in 2024.

134
00:04:50,379 --> 00:04:51,974
About eighty percent of all the

135
00:04:51,994 --> 00:04:53,971
pollination money in the country.

136
00:04:54,311 --> 00:04:55,101
One crop.

137
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:56,011
One valley.

138
00:04:56,121 --> 00:04:58,041
Almost every hive in America.

139
00:04:59,931 --> 00:05:00,831
Chapter five.

140
00:05:00,851 --> 00:05:01,911
The health bill.

141
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:05,041
So what would losing all of them really cost us?

142
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:06,081
Not in money.

143
00:05:06,141 --> 00:05:07,014
In health.

144
00:05:07,354 --> 00:05:11,544
In 2015, a team of researchers modelled exactly that:

145
00:05:11,864 --> 00:05:14,544
a world where every animal pollinator is gone.

146
00:05:14,694 --> 00:05:15,864
Not just bees.

147
00:05:15,884 --> 00:05:16,759
All of them.

148
00:05:17,089 --> 00:05:20,762
Global fruit supplies fell by about twenty-three percent.

149
00:05:20,782 --> 00:05:22,204
Vegetables, by sixteen.

150
00:05:22,224 --> 00:05:24,477
Nuts and seeds, by twenty-two.

151
00:05:24,807 --> 00:05:28,347
Seventy-one million more people could become short of vitamin A.

152
00:05:28,697 --> 00:05:31,087
A hundred and seventy-three million more,

153
00:05:31,127 --> 00:05:32,237
short of folate.

154
00:05:32,567 --> 00:05:34,652
And that's on top of the two point two

155
00:05:34,672 --> 00:05:37,801
billion people who already don't get enough vitamin A.

156
00:05:38,141 --> 00:05:40,451
And with fewer fruits and vegetables on the plate,

157
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:42,471
heart disease, strokes,

158
00:05:42,491 --> 00:05:44,985
diabetes and some cancers would rise.

159
00:05:45,315 --> 00:05:46,169
Their estimate:

160
00:05:46,189 --> 00:05:49,356
about one point four million extra deaths every year.

161
00:05:49,376 --> 00:05:52,255
Almost three percent more deaths, worldwide.

162
00:05:52,585 --> 00:05:53,355
It's a model,

163
00:05:53,375 --> 00:05:54,895
built on big assumptions.

164
00:05:55,035 --> 00:05:56,624
But the direction is clear.

165
00:05:56,984 --> 00:05:58,754
Here's the uncomfortable part.

166
00:05:59,124 --> 00:06:03,010
A follow-up study in 2022 estimated that poor pollination is

167
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:07,074
already costing three to five percent of the world's fruit,

168
00:06:07,204 --> 00:06:08,907
vegetables and nuts.

169
00:06:09,257 --> 00:06:11,315
That loss is linked to about four hundred

170
00:06:11,335 --> 00:06:13,787
and twenty-seven thousand excess deaths a year.

171
00:06:14,017 --> 00:06:14,987
Not someday.

172
00:06:15,167 --> 00:06:15,857
Now.

173
00:06:17,827 --> 00:06:18,827
Chapter six.

174
00:06:18,847 --> 00:06:20,105
Who else is out there?

175
00:06:20,795 --> 00:06:21,595
But wait.

176
00:06:21,705 --> 00:06:23,395
Our question was about bees.

177
00:06:23,495 --> 00:06:25,805
And bees are not the only pollinators.

178
00:06:26,135 --> 00:06:27,115
Take chocolate.

179
00:06:27,375 --> 00:06:30,945
The cacao tree's tiny flowers are pollinated by midges.

180
00:06:31,175 --> 00:06:33,737
Flies just one to three millimetres long.

181
00:06:34,067 --> 00:06:35,557
So in a world without bees,

182
00:06:35,577 --> 00:06:36,947
chocolate would survive.

183
00:06:37,077 --> 00:06:37,956
Probably.

184
00:06:38,346 --> 00:06:41,971
Flies, beetles, moths and butterflies visit flowers too.

185
00:06:41,991 --> 00:06:43,559
Hummingbirds sip nectar.

186
00:06:43,579 --> 00:06:44,500
And at night,

187
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:45,911
bats pollinate agave,

188
00:06:45,931 --> 00:06:47,978
the plant behind tequila.

189
00:06:48,308 --> 00:06:50,808
But none of them do the job quite like bees.

190
00:06:50,988 --> 00:06:52,958
Bees feed their young on pollen.

191
00:06:53,108 --> 00:06:55,248
Their whole bodies are built to collect it.

192
00:06:55,598 --> 00:06:57,661
Losing them wouldn't end life on Earth.

193
00:06:57,681 --> 00:07:00,449
It would make it smaller, plainer, and harder.

194
00:07:02,229 --> 00:07:03,219
Chapter seven.

195
00:07:03,239 --> 00:07:04,411
The real news.

196
00:07:05,111 --> 00:07:07,261
Here's a number that is easy to miss.

197
00:07:07,611 --> 00:07:09,121
In the last fifty years,

198
00:07:09,211 --> 00:07:11,856
the amount of food we grow from pollinator-dependent

199
00:07:11,876 --> 00:07:14,391
crops has gone up by three hundred percent.

200
00:07:14,631 --> 00:07:16,106
We need them more than ever.

201
00:07:16,456 --> 00:07:17,995
So, are bees disappearing?

202
00:07:18,015 --> 00:07:19,070
Not all at once.

203
00:07:19,090 --> 00:07:21,167
Honeybees are kept like livestock,

204
00:07:21,187 --> 00:07:24,357
and beekeepers replace the colonies they lose.

205
00:07:24,697 --> 00:07:26,937
The real worry is the wild ones.

206
00:07:27,267 --> 00:07:29,027
Where scientists have assessed them,

207
00:07:29,137 --> 00:07:32,457
more than forty percent of invertebrate pollinator species,

208
00:07:32,527 --> 00:07:35,766
mostly bees and butterflies, face extinction.

209
00:07:36,086 --> 00:07:38,986
The causes are the ones Maoxian learned the hard way.

210
00:07:39,316 --> 00:07:42,006
Lost habitat, heavy pesticide use,

211
00:07:42,046 --> 00:07:43,505
and a changing climate.

212
00:07:43,835 --> 00:07:45,985
And the fixes are surprisingly simple.

213
00:07:46,155 --> 00:07:48,485
Strips of wildflowers along the fields.

214
00:07:48,635 --> 00:07:50,435
Fewer, smarter sprays.

215
00:07:50,655 --> 00:07:53,037
A corner of the garden left a little wild.

216
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:56,467
Einstein never warned us about bees.

217
00:07:56,647 --> 00:07:58,803
But the quote sticks around for a reason.

218
00:07:59,143 --> 00:08:00,583
So many of the apples,

219
00:08:00,723 --> 00:08:02,517
almonds and bright colours on your

220
00:08:02,537 --> 00:08:04,622
plate began with a tiny delivery.

221
00:08:05,462 --> 00:08:08,662
Next time: something in the sky we take for granted.

222
00:08:08,872 --> 00:08:10,579
What if the Moon disappeared?

223
00:08:11,529 --> 00:08:12,592
This is Whyverse.

224
00:08:12,612 --> 00:08:14,487
Big questions, visual answers.

